# Endings: checked in this order on arrival day; the last one has no conditions.
# Each ending is one carrier clause (the fleet's hail vote) times one arrival clause
# (the storm vote: held the plan, waited it out in orbit, or no storm). Every ending
# closes on the first crate. `title` is the heading; see STORY_BIBLE.md.

card end_lights
type ending
cat ending
title Lights On
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_hold
text Two kilometres up, in the brown, the cameras show nothing but the bearing line Cargo 2's radio draws. Then the brown starts to glow white from underneath. The board cleared one more command at midnight, and the one who answered sent it: lights. Cargo 2 is spending its saved battery on every floodlight it has. Seven ships turn for the glow and set down one by one on the flat, three kilometres short, each blind in its own plume at the last. The first crate out of Cargo 2's hold says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_through
type ending
cat ending
title Through the Dust
when flag arrive_hold
text The seventh ship is the one the gust caught. It stands upright on the slope three kilometres from Cargo 2, a leg strut folded under it, and the first walk-round says what the readouts said: it will not fly home. Its pilot sits on the bottom rung for a long minute, then starts a list of what it can still be: habitat, store, radio mast. Two sols later the first suit out, who lost the vote to answer, climbs the rim, counts the dark between flares, and answers from two hundred metres, inside the tick. HELLO RX · OK. To Starbase: "Landed. Seven down, six to fly. Found the eighth."

card end_long_orbit
type ending
cat ending
title The Long Orbit
when flag signal_answered
when flag arrive_wait
text Week three in the long orbit, night pass, half rations on seven galley tables. Below, the storm; on the night side, one lamp, steady since the handshake. Cargo 2's manifest comes up a line a pass, read aloud in turn: tank liners, a glove, tyres. Tonight's line is a drill bit, and seven hungry people cheer a drill bit. A week later the storm clears. On the way down a tile on one hinge line runs hotter than the brief allowed, and holds, and seven ships land. The first crate the rover brings back says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_patience
type ending
cat ending
title Patience
when flag arrive_wait
text Dawn on the rim, frost on the wing. The first suit out, who lost the vote to answer, kneels at Cargo 2's service port with a battery pack and a radio,, counting aloud: the strobe has been dark since midnight. Clip, latch, wait. Nine. Ten. Eleven. A click, slow, like something clearing its throat. The answer goes in by hand, from two metres, inside the tick. HELLO RX · OK. Behind them stand seven ships that waited out two weeks of storm in the long orbit on half rations, under the ground's hold. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth."

card end_friend
type ending
cat ending
title Old Friend
when flag signal_answered
text Second sol, thirty kilometres out, the steady light ahead hasn't moved in an hour. The one who packed the bag drives with one eye on the battery, already past half. Two kilometres out, Cargo 2 hears the rover and runs the pad crew's arrival routine, written for a truck from the hangar: floods, strobe, hatch lamp, across the empty plain. The driver laughs out loud, alone in the cab, hooks the last spare battery to its port, and drives home slow. The first crate says, in Sharpie: "You'll thank me." To Starbase: "Landed. All eight."

card end_close
type ending
cat ending
title Close Enough
text Third sol, two hundred metres from Cargo 2, the rover parked nose to the strobe. On the first sol Cargo 3's drill snapped its only bit, and the water margin has shrunk daily since; the spare is in that hold. The one who packed a bag for this drive counts the dark: nine, ten, eleven, flare, and answers. HELLO RX · OK. Then Cargo 2 does what flight mode says, every heater on at once, and the strobe goes out, and stays out: two years of asking, and the battery had one answer left. The bit is where the manifest said. To Starbase: "Landed. All seven. Found the eighth, in time."
